## 2. Official-product use is separate from source reuse

An "Official Product" is an application or executable distribution that the
Owner intentionally offers to end users through an Owner-designated site,
store, or delivery channel. Source-repository availability is not itself an
Official Product offering. Installation instructions, example commands, a
missing checkout, or access to a file are not an independent grant to build,
run, self-host, or redistribute the source.

Subject to the actual offering, these terms, and any separately agreed product
terms, the Owner grants a nonexclusive, nontransferable, nonsublicensable right
to operate the unmodified Official Product for its documented purpose within
the scope of your end-user entitlement:

- **Expressly free offering:** you may use the functionality the Owner
  intentionally offers without a charge, within the stated users, edition,
  channel, duration, and other limits. Absence of a listed price by itself is
  not permission to use repository code or an unoffered product.
- **Priced offering:** the applicable purchase, subscription, or express written
  exception is required before using the priced functionality. A separately
  offered free trial or free edition is limited to its stated scope.
- **Changes to an offering:** the Owner may end or change a free offering for
  future access or use by communicating the new terms and their effective date.
  This does not impose retroactive fees, authorize a charge without agreement,
  cancel an existing valid paid entitlement, or revoke rights preserved by
  section 7. Perpetual purchases remain perpetual for their purchased scope;
  term-based access follows the agreed term. Updates are included only to the
  extent the applicable offer says they are.

This limited operating permission includes the loading, browser caching, and
installation copies technically necessary for authorized use and any backup
copy expressly allowed by the applicable product terms or law. It is not a
permission to create a separate deployment, distribute the product, or use its
implementation in another product. A payment, subscription, donation, or free
access never by itself purchases source-code rights.

